const List<TechniqueSection> techSpeedBurstsLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why",
    body:
        "Speed is built in short bursts, not by playing fast until it hurts. A burst lets you play a little faster than your comfortable tempo for a moment, and the rest right after it teaches the most important skill at speed: letting go. Over weeks the bursts get longer and the tempo climbs.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How",
    body:
        "Low sticks (about 5 to 8 cm), loose grip, the stick rebounding on every note; at speed the fingers take over more of the work from the wrists. The burst starts relaxed, not with a jolt, and the landing note is part of it. In the rest, deliberately drop the shoulders and soften the hands before the next burst.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Tips",
    body:
        "Only go as fast as the bursts stay even; uneven fast notes are not faster, just messier. If the forearms burn, the grip is too tight or the sticks too high. Line 4 gives you a whole bar of rest after each burst, line 5 a landing note and three beats of rest: bar of rest: use it. Line 8 is two bars without a break, the test of everything before it.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Song examples",
    body:
        "In the Air Tonight (Phil Collins): one famous sixteenth-note burst around the toms, then the groove. Tom Sawyer (Rush): Neil Peart's quick fills between steady grooves. Smells Like Teen Spirit (Nirvana): the burst of a fill that kicks off the song.",
  ),
